  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Given that ${\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=4$ and ${\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=6,$ then what is ${\\int }_{-2}^{3}(f(x)-6)\\,\\text{d}x?$",
  "worked_solution": "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information into the above gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4+6 & ={\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 10 & ={\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the desired integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}(f(x)-6)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x-6{\\int }_{-2}^{3}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10-6\\cdot x\\,{|}_{-2}^{3} \\\\  & =10-6(3-(-2)) \\\\  & =10-6(5) \\\\  & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
